{"reference":"Amos 6","book":"Amos","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Social Critique","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter attacks people portrayed as secure, luxurious, and unconcerned about Joseph’s affliction. Prosperity under Jeroboam II and the book’s accusations of inequality offer a plausible social horizon for this rhetoric, but the prophetic language should not be treated as a census or a complete record of economic conditions.","verse_refs":["Amos 6:1-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_006_ca4891e73b9606cd0ea46f50"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}